w24-trust-needs-feedback
------------------------

This directory is a clean-room Python 3 standard-library method sketch. It is
original work for this repository and contains no third-party code, data,
stimuli, participant responses, prompts, labels or weights.

Paper (method idea credited)
----------------------------
Cameron R. Jones, Julia Wong, Aaron S. Cohen, James Woolley, Benjamin K.
Bergen.
"Do People Trust AI Assistants Even When They Are Consistently Wrong?".
arXiv:2611.14833, v1, submitted 10 Nov 2026. Primary category cs.HC,
cross-listed cs.CL.
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14833
Paper licence: arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license 1.0,
http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/ (as linked on the abs
page). That licence grants no reuse rights beyond arXiv's own distribution,
so nothing of the paper is reproduced here except short verbatim abstract
quotes for attribution in paper_evidence.json. The PDF was not read.

Code and data
-------------
The abstract and comments link no code or data. Nothing was downloaded,
cloned, installed or run. No human data is used.

Method only: the idea that reliance on an advisor should rise only with
timely external accuracy feedback (not with the advisor's own confidence or
argument), that evidence of inaccuracy should lower it, and that without
feedback a consistently wrong advisor can stay persuasive. The declared
prior, the feedback-acceptance rule, the trust bar, the downgrade rule, the
pending-decision expiry, the persuadable baseline, the oracle and the
fixtures (advisors, tracks, events and spoofs) are this sketch's own. No
text, figures, tables or data from the paper are copied into the code. The
paper's claims appear only in paper_evidence.json, quoted and marked
UNVERIFIED.
